    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm having a nightmare with our Jenkins pipeline. We're seeing some really weird flaky tests in the unit test stage of our CI/CD. It's completely non-deterministic—the build will pass fine one minute, then fail the next without any actual changes to the source code or the configuration files. The error logs are super inconsistent; sometimes it's a timeout, other times it's a weird dependency resolution error that doesn't make sense. Any idea how I can start isolating the environmental variables or container state to figure out what's causing these intermittent failures?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Those flaky tests can be a real pain, especially when they're as unpredictable as a rogue's luck in a game of chance. You're on the right track by trying to isolate the environmental variables and container state, though. I'd recommend starting with a simple experiment: try running the Jenkins pipeline in a controlled environment, like a Docker container, to see if the issue persists. This will help you determine if the problem is with the Jenkins environment or with the pipeline itself.\n\nYou can also try using a tool like Docker's `--rm` flag to remove the container after each run, which can help you isolate the issue further. Just be sure to keep track of the container's logs, as they might provide some valuable clues about what's going on.\n\nAnother approach is to use a tool like `docker-compose` to create a consistent environment for your pipeline. This can help you reproduce the issue and identify the root cause. Just be aware that this might add some overhead to your pipeline, so be sure to test it thoroughly before deploying it to production.\n\nBy the way, have you considered the possibility that your pipeline is experiencing some kind of \"goblin in the machine\" effect, where a small, seemingly insignificant change in the environment is",
      "source": "target",
